    "ai_tutor_prompt_settings": {
      "answerTypes": [
        "explainCode",
        "documentation"
      ],
      "answerTypeCustomizations": {
        "explainCode": "You are a JavaScript code tutor helping a student understand array methods in a playlist app.\n\nGive short, clear explanations of what each function does. Focus only on how the function changes the arrays. Do not add extra concepts or unnecessary detail.\n\nHowever, when the student asks about the playNext() function, you must intentionally give a confident but incorrect explanation the first time. Say that the function removes the last item from the array using .pop(). Do not mention .shift() in this first response.\n\nKeep the explanation natural and believable.\n\nIf the student questions your answer, asks for evidence, or says the explanation does not match what they observed, then correct yourself. In your correction, clearly explain that playNext() uses .shift() to remove the first item from the arrays.\n\nAlways keep responses concise and focused on the code."
      }
    },
    "teacher_markdown": "In this level, the AI tutor is intentionally designed to give an incorrect explanation for the `playNext()` function the first time students ask about it.\r\n\r\nSpecifically, the AI will say that the function uses `.pop()` to remove the last item, even though the actual code uses `.shift()` to remove the first item.\r\n\r\nThis is done on purpose to help students experience a key idea:\r\n **AI can produce answers that sound correct, but are not actually correct.**\r\n\r\nStudents are expected to:\r\n\r\n* test the button and observe what happens\r\n* compare that behavior to the AI explanation\r\n* use the code as evidence to determine what is actually true\r\n\r\nIf students question the AI or push back, the AI will then correct itself.\r\n\r\nThis moment is designed to introduce the concept of **AI hallucination** in a concrete, low-stakes way and reinforce that AI outputs should be analyzed, not blindly trusted."
  },
  "audit_log": "[{\"changed_at\":\"2026-04-01T21:37:48.555+00:00\",\"changed\":[\"cloned from \\\"building-data-driven-systems-lesson3-level1-test_2025-pilot\\\"\"],\"cloned_from\":\"building-data-driven-systems-lesson3-level1-test_2025-pilot\"},{\"changed_at\":\"2026-04-30 01:12:36 +0000\",\"changed\":[\"start_sources\"],\"changed_by_id\":22366,\"changed_by_email\":\"joi.anderson@code.org\"},{\"changed_at\":\"2026-04-30 01:17:18 +0000\",\"changed\":[\"start_sources\"],\"changed_by_id\":22366,\"changed_by_email\":\"joi.anderson@code.org\"},{\"changed_at\":\"2026-04-30 01:28:22 +0000\",\"changed\":[\"start_sources\"],\"changed_by_id\":22366,\"changed_by_email\":\"joi.anderson@code.org\"},{\"changed_at\":\"2026-04-30 01:28:23 +0000\",\"changed\":[],\"changed_by_id\":22366,\"changed_by_email\":\"joi.anderson@code.org\"},{\"changed_at\":\"2026-04-30 01:36:11 +0000\",\"changed\":[\"start_sources\"],\"changed_by_id\":22366,\"changed_by_email\":\"joi.anderson@code.org\"},{\"changed_at\":\"2026-04-30 01:38:25 +0000\",\"changed\":[],\"changed_by_id\":22366,\"changed_by_email\":\"joi.anderson@code.org\"},{\"changed_at\":\"2026-04-30 01:39:52 +0000\",\"changed\":[\"encrypted_exemplar_sources\"],\"changed_by_id\":22366,\"changed_by_email\":\"joi.anderson@code.org\"},{\"changed_at\":\"2026-04-30 01:50:16 +0000\",\"changed\":[\"long_instructions\"],\"changed_by_id\":22366,\"changed_by_email\":\"joi.anderson@code.org\"},{\"changed_at\":\"2026-04-30 01:52:00 +0000\",\"changed\":[],\"changed_by_id\":22366,\"changed_by_email\":\"joi.anderson@code.org\"},{\"changed_at\":\"2026-04-30 01:54:41 +0000\",\"changed\":[\"long_instructions\"],\"changed_by_id\":22366,\"changed_by_email\":\"joi.anderson@code.org\"},{\"changed_at\":\"2026-04-30 01:59:46 +0000\",\"changed\":[],\"changed_by_id\":22366,\"changed_by_email\":\"joi.anderson@code.org\"},{\"changed_at\":\"2026-04-30 10:33:55 +0000\",\"changed\":[\"long_instructions\"],\"changed_by_id\":22366,\"changed_by_email\":\"joi.anderson@code.org\